Lower performance was observed for several less-represented categories, particularly fallacy of credibility and equivocation.


10. Selected Fallacy Detection Model

The selected model is:

Model: Linear SVM
Features: Word TF-IDF + Character TF-IDF
Word n-grams: (1,2)
Character n-grams: (3,5)
Class weighting: Balanced

The model was saved as:

models/
└── logic_tfidf_svm_improved.pkl

This model was selected based on validation performance and was subsequently evaluated on the held-out test set.


11. Final LOGIC Test Evaluation

After model comparison, the Word + Character TF-IDF Linear SVM was selected using the validation set. The held-out test set was then used for final evaluation.

The final test set contained 286 examples.

Final Test Performance

Metric                 Score
Accuracy               57.34%
Macro Precision        57.86%
Macro Recall           53.31%
Macro F1               52.72%
Weighted F1            56.23%

The strongest individual categories on the test set were:

Fallacy                  F1
False dilemma            75.86%
Ad populum               75.41%
Ad hominem               71.60%
False causality          63.16%
Faulty generalization    58.12%
Circular reasoning       55.81%

Lower performance was observed for:

Equivocation — 33.33%
Fallacy of logic — 33.33%
Fallacy of relevance — 34.29%
Fallacy of credibility — 35.71%

The relatively low performance for some categories is partly associated with their limited representation in the validation/test sets and semantic overlap between certain fallacy types.

The test set was not used for model selection or hyperparameter tuning.

The current LOGIC classifier should therefore be treated as an assistive analytical component rather than an authoritative judge of whether an argument contains a fallacy.


12. LOGIC Interpretation of Results

The experiments demonstrate that the representation used for text classification has a substantial effect on fallacy-detection performance.

The initial Logistic Regression model achieved a validation Macro F1 of 40.14%. Replacing it with a Linear SVM and combining word-level and character-level TF-IDF features increased validation Macro F1 to 51.74%.

The final held-out test evaluation produced a Macro F1 of 52.72% and an accuracy of 57.34%.

The results indicate that the model can identify several fallacy categories effectively, particularly:

False dilemma
Ad populum
Ad hominem
False causality
Faulty generalization

However, performance remains weaker for some categories with fewer examples or stronger semantic similarity to other fallacies.


13. Argument Component Detection: AAEC

Following the LOGIC experiments, the project was extended to argument-component detection using the Argument Annotated Essays Corpus (AAEC).

The AAEC dataset contains 402 annotated persuasive essays.

The BRAT annotations were parsed to extract:

MajorClaim
Claim
Premise

The official essay-level split was preserved:

322 training essays
80 test essays

The initial preprocessing produced 6,089 argument-component instances.

Two exact text duplicates were found across the training and test sets. The duplicate test instances were removed to prevent leakage.

The resulting dataset contained:

6,087 samples
4,823 training samples
1,264 test samples

Final class distribution:

Class          Samples
Premise        3,832
Claim          1,506
MajorClaim       749

The cleaned dataset was stored as:

datasets/
└── processed/
    └── aaec_components_clean.csv


