21. Explainable Bias-Aware Analysis

The BiasLess bias component will not treat the classifier as a definitive detector of a person's or argument's bias.

The analysis combines transparent indicators, including:

Loaded or evaluative language
Emotionally charged wording
Extremity or exaggeration
Subjective language
Stereotyping or broad generalization patterns
Sentiment intensity
Potentially bias-associated wording identified by the trained WNC classifier

The final system can provide feedback such as:

Potentially loaded wording detected.

Flagged term:
"controversial"

Reason:

Evaluative wording associated with the neutralized revision.

This approach is intended to make the system's feedback more interpretable and suitable for debate analysis.


22. Sentiment Analysis

Sentiment analysis was implemented using the open-source pretrained model:

cardiffnlp/twitter-roberta-base-sentiment-latest

The model produces three sentiment classes:

Positive
Neutral
Negative

Unlike the LOGIC, AAEC, and WNC classifiers, this component is not trained from scratch for BiasLess. A pretrained open-source model is integrated locally.

The model was tested on debate-style statements and successfully produced sentiment labels with confidence scores.

Example results included:

"I completely agree with this argument."
→ Positive
→ Confidence: 0.8527

"This policy is absolutely terrible."
→ Negative
→ Confidence: 0.9387

"The evidence supports the proposed solution."
→ Neutral
→ Confidence: 0.7161

"I am not convinced by your argument."
→ Negative
→ Confidence: 0.7323

"This is a reasonable approach."
→ Positive
→ Confidence: 0.6008

"That claim is completely ridiculous."
→ Negative
→ Confidence: 0.9202

"There are both advantages and disadvantages to this approach."
→ Neutral
→ Confidence: 0.7843

The sentiment component is used as a supporting linguistic signal. It is not treated as a direct measure of argument quality or bias.

The reusable implementation is:

C:\Users\Rasika\BiasLess\backend\nlp\sentiment.py


23. Named Entity Recognition

Named Entity Recognition was added as a supporting NLP component for identifying important entities within debate statements.

The implementation uses the open-source pretrained model:

dslim/bert-base-NER

The model is integrated locally using the Hugging Face Transformers library.

The NER model identifies entity categories such as:

PER  → Person
ORG  → Organization
LOC  → Location
MISC → Miscellaneous

Example:

Input:

"According to WHO, India needs stronger healthcare policies."

Detected entities:

WHO
→ ORG

India
→ LOC

Another example:

"Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella spoke at an event in Mumbai."

Detected entities:

Microsoft
→ ORG

Satya Nadella
→ PER

Mumbai
→ LOC

The model is used directly without fine-tuning on a BiasLess-specific NER dataset.

Custom post-processing was added to improve entity-span handling. For example, the pretrained model initially returned the name "Elon Musk" as fragmented predictions:

El
→ ORG

on Musk
→ PER

The custom post-processing combines the overlapping entity spans and produces:

Elon Musk
→ PER

This post-processing changes the model output representation but does not modify the pretrained model weights.

Therefore, the NER component is a pretrained model with custom rule-based output processing, not a fine-tuned model.

The reusable implementation is:

C:\Users\Rasika\BiasLess\backend\nlp\ner.py

NER is used as a supporting component for later evidence and argument analysis.


24. Evidence Extraction

Evidence extraction was implemented as a rule-based NLP component that combines NER results with linguistic and numerical patterns.

The purpose of the component is to identify signals that a speaker is presenting or referring to evidence.

The component does not verify whether the presented evidence is factually correct.

The evidence extraction pipeline is:

Debate Statement
       ↓
NER
       ↓
Source Detection
       ↓
Statistic Detection
       ↓
Date Detection
       ↓
Evidence Indicator Detection
       ↓
Evidence Presence Score


24.1 Evidence Indicators

The extractor identifies phrases such as:

according to
research shows
research suggests
study found
study shows
studies show
data shows
data suggests
evidence shows
evidence suggests
report states
report says
survey found
survey shows
experts say
scientists say
scientists found
researchers found
analysis shows
statistics show


24.2 Source Detection

The extractor identifies source patterns such as:

according to WHO
research from Oxford University
study from Oxford University
report by Microsoft
reported by an organization
published by an institution

For example:

"A 2024 study from Oxford University found that renewable energy reduced emissions."

The extractor identifies:

Source:
Oxford University

Date:
2024

The source extraction patterns were adjusted so that only the likely source name is extracted instead of the remaining part of the sentence.


24.3 Statistical Information

The extractor identifies numerical evidence patterns including:

40%
75 percent
10 million
5 billion
₹500 crore
$2 million

For example:

"Research shows that 75 percent of students prefer online learning."

The extractor identifies:

Statistic:
75 percent

Evidence indicator:
research shows


24.4 Date Detection

The extractor detects years and common date formats.

Examples:

2024
2020
15 September 2026
September 15, 2026

It can also detect multiple years within a sentence.

For example:

"Global temperatures increased between 2015 and 2020."

Detected dates:

2015
2020


24.5 Evidence Presence Score

The extractor calculates an explainable Evidence Presence Score.

The current heuristic assigns:

Evidence indicator present:
+0.30

Explicit source present:
+0.25

Statistic present:
+0.20

Date present:
+0.10

Person or organization entity present:
+0.15

The final score is limited to a maximum of 1.0.

The score represents the presence of evidence-related linguistic signals.

It does not represent:

Factual correctness
Source reliability
Truthfulness
Argument validity
Scientific strength of evidence


24.6 Example Evidence Analysis

Input:

"According to WHO, India reduced malaria cases by 40% in 2020."

Output:

Source:
WHO

Entity:
India → LOC

Statistic:
40%

Date:
2020

Evidence indicator:
according to

Evidence Presence Score:
1.0

Evidence Bearing:
True


Another example:

"A 2024 study from Oxford University found that renewable energy reduced emissions."

Output:

Source:
Oxford University

Date:
2024

Entity:
Oxford University → ORG

Evidence Presence Score:
0.5

Evidence Bearing:
True


Another example:

"The government announced a new education policy."

Output:

Evidence Presence Score:
0.0

Evidence Bearing:
False

No source, statistic, date, or explicit evidence indicator was detected.


24.7 Evidence Extraction Implementation

The reusable implementation is:

C:\Users\Rasika\BiasLess\backend\nlp\evidence.py

The evidence extractor currently uses:

NER
Regular expressions
Source patterns
Evidence indicator matching
Date detection
Statistical pattern detection
Explainable scoring rules

No external API is required for this component.


25. Current BiasLess NLP Architecture

At the current stage, BiasLess contains three custom-trained classification components and several supporting pretrained and rule-based NLP components.

The current NLP components are:

Fallacy Detection – LOGIC Education dataset using Word + Character TF-IDF with Linear SVM.
Argument Component Detection – AAEC v2 dataset using Word + Character TF-IDF with Linear SVM.
Bias-Aware Language Detection – Wikipedia Neutrality Corpus using Word + Character TF-IDF with Linear SVM.
Sentiment Analysis – pretrained RoBERTa sentiment model.
Named Entity Recognition – pretrained BERT NER model with custom entity-span post-processing.
Evidence Extraction – rule-based component combining NER and linguistic patterns.
Argument Quality Analysis – explainable heuristic scoring layer combining the outputs of the NLP components.

The current architecture is:

                         Debate Utterance
                                │
             ┌──────────────────┼──────────────────┐
             ↓                  ↓                  ↓
      Argument Component      Fallacy          Bias-Aware
          Classifier         Detection          Language
                              Classifier         Analysis
             │                  │                  │
             ↓                  ↓                  ↓
       MajorClaim /        13 Fallacy       Neutral /
       Claim / Premise      Categories      Bias-associated
                                               signal
             │                  │                  │
             └──────────────────┼──────────────────┘
                                ↓
                         Sentiment Analysis
                                ↓
                       Named Entity Recognition
                                ↓
                         Evidence Extraction
                                ↓
                        Argument Quality Score
                                ↓
                       Explainable Feedback

The system therefore combines supervised machine-learning models with pretrained NLP models and rule-based analysis.

