30. Integrated BiasLess NLP Engine

After completing the individual NLP modules, they were integrated into a unified NLP engine.

The implementation is:

C:\Users\Rasika\BiasLess\backend\nlp\engine.py

The purpose of the engine is to provide one common interface for analyzing a debate utterance instead of requiring the backend to call every NLP component independently.


30.1 Integrated Components

The current engine integrates:

Argument Component Detection
Fallacy Detection
Bias-Aware Language Detection
Sentiment Analysis
Named Entity Recognition
Evidence Extraction
Argument Quality Analysis
Counterargument Generation

The integrated flow is:

                         Debate Utterance
                                │
             ┌──────────────────┼──────────────────┐
             ↓                  ↓                  ↓
      Argument Component      Fallacy          Bias-Aware
          Detection          Detection          Language
             │                  │               Detection
             └──────────────────┼──────────────────┘
                                ↓
                         Sentiment Analysis
                                ↓
                    Named Entity Recognition
                                ↓
                       Evidence Extraction
                                ↓
                       Specificity Analysis
                                ↓
                    Argument Quality Analysis
                                ↓
                  Counterargument Generation
                                ↓
                     Structured NLP Result


30.2 Output Normalization

During integration, the outputs produced by different NLP modules required normalization because individual components use different internal result structures.

The engine therefore includes normalization logic for:

Argument component output
Fallacy output
Bias-aware output
Sentiment output
Evidence output
Argument-quality output

This creates a consistent representation that can later be transmitted through the FastAPI backend and WebSocket connection.


30.3 SVM Decision Strength

The trained Linear SVM classifiers do not naturally produce probability values.

Therefore, their raw decision-function margins are not presented as probabilities.

For internal review-oriented scoring, BiasLess converts the absolute SVM margin into a bounded decision-strength indicator:

decision_strength = |margin| / (1 + |margin|)

The resulting value lies between 0 and 1.

This value is called Decision Strength rather than Confidence or Probability because it is derived from the SVM decision margin and is not a calibrated probability.


