33.2 Voice Activity Detection

Voice Activity Detection has been implemented using:

Silero VAD

The purpose of the VAD component is to identify regions containing speech before passing them to the speech-to-text system.

The implementation supports:

- WAV audio input
- Stereo-to-mono conversion
- Sample-rate normalization
- 48 kHz to 16 kHz resampling
- Local Silero VAD inference
- Timestamped speech-region detection

The reusable implementation is:

C:\Users\Rasika\BiasLess\backend\speech\vad.py

The current VAD pipeline is:

Input WAV
    ↓
Read PCM Audio
    ↓
Stereo → Mono
    ↓
Resample to 16 kHz
    ↓
Silero VAD
    ↓
Speech Timestamps


33.3 VAD Verification

The VAD component was tested using:

C:\Users\Rasika\BiasLess\tests\sample_audio.wav

The original sample audio was:

- Stereo
- 48 kHz
- 16-bit PCM

The VAD implementation automatically converted the audio to:

- Mono
- 16 kHz
- Float audio representation

The test successfully detected two speech-activity regions:

Segment 1:
1.12s - 4.13s
Duration: 3.00s

Segment 2:
5.19s - 5.57s
Duration: 0.38s

This confirms that Silero VAD is successfully identifying speech regions from the input audio.

The VAD component is therefore considered implemented and verified.

The VAD output represents detected speech activity and does not by itself determine whether the detected audio contains meaningful debate content.


33.4 Speech-to-Text and VAD Relationship

The current speech components operate as separate verified modules.

The intended integrated pipeline is:

Audio
    ↓
Silero VAD
    ↓
Detected Speech Region
    ↓
Speech Segment Extraction
    ↓
faster-whisper
    ↓
Timestamped Text
    ↓
BiasLess NLP Engine

This integration will prevent unnecessary transcription of silence or non-speech regions and will allow the NLP engine to process completed speech utterances.


33.5 Speaker Handling

The initial implementation will use separate microphones or input devices for Speaker A and Speaker B where practical.

This approach provides a simpler method for associating detected speech with individual debate participants.

The initial system does not require automatic speaker diarization.

Automatic speaker diarization can be investigated as future enhancement after the core near-real-time pipeline is completed.


33.6 Near-Real-Time Processing Strategy

The system will process speech in short chunks rather than waiting until the complete debate has finished.

The intended sequence is:

Capture Audio
        ↓
Detect Speech
        ↓
Extract Speech Region
        ↓
Transcribe Speech
        ↓
Buffer Text
        ↓
Identify Completed Utterance
        ↓
Run BiasLess NLP Engine
        ↓
Generate Structured Analysis
        ↓
Send Result to Dashboard

The system should therefore be described as a near-real-time debate analysis system.

The term near-real-time is used because speech recognition and NLP processing require computational time and the system does not guarantee instantaneous analysis.


