37. Current Limitations

The current implementation has several limitations that should be explicitly acknowledged.

The fallacy classifier achieved 57.34% test accuracy and 52.72% Macro F1. Therefore, its predictions may contain false positives and false negatives.

The argument-component classifier performs substantially better on Premise detection than on Claim and MajorClaim classification.

The WNC classifier identifies wording patterns associated with Wikipedia neutrality revisions. Its output should not be interpreted as objective proof that a person or statement is biased.

The sentiment model is a pretrained general-purpose supporting component and has not been evaluated on a dedicated BiasLess debate sentiment test set.

The NER model is pretrained and has not been fine-tuned specifically for debate transcripts.

The Evidence Presence Score detects linguistic evidence signals but does not verify whether a claim, statistic, source, or statement is factually correct.

The Argument Quality Score is an explainable heuristic score rather than a learned objective measurement of argument quality.

The SVM Decision Strength values are transformations of decision margins and are not calibrated probabilities.

The counterargument generator is currently template-based, so its responses are more controlled and explainable but less flexible than responses generated by a large language model.

The current VAD implementation has been verified on WAV audio input. Browser microphone streaming and continuous chunk-based processing have not yet been integrated.

The faster-whisper component has been verified independently on sample audio, but the complete VAD → speech-segment extraction → Whisper → NLP pipeline is still under integration.

Automatic speaker diarization has not yet been implemented.

FastAPI, WebSocket communication, and the React dashboard have not yet been integrated with the speech pipeline.

These limitations are important because BiasLess is designed as an assistive debate-analysis system rather than an automated authority on argument correctness.


